id: recipe.distributed-worker-pool
kind: recipe
title: Distributed worker pool
aliases: distributed worker pool, consumers across nodes, scaled workers
question: how would you run a worker pool across many nodes with a shared queue?
tags: architecture, batch, scaling
properties:
  - worker replicas scale with backlog
  - one item is owned by exactly one worker at a time
  - poison items do not block the pipeline
  - deploys drain instead of dropping work
invariant: Ownership is held through a visibility timeout or lease, so concurrency is bounded per item while replicas scale freely.
answer_30s: Workers pull from a shared durable queue with a visibility lease (so an unacknowledged item is redelivered), bound their own concurrency, and are scaled on backlog rather than CPU.
flow: |
  durable queue (visibility timeout = lease)
     |
  worker deployment (N replicas, each with bounded concurrency)
     |
  per-item: acquire lease -> process idempotently -> ack
     |
  overdue lease -> redelivery (retry count bounded) -> dead letter
compose:
  - design.worker-pool => each replica runs a fixed, reasoned concurrency
  - design.bounded-queue => backlog bounded by shed or backpressure at the producer
  - design.idempotency => lease expiry means the item can be processed twice
  - design.retry-backoff => retries before dead-lettering, with jitter
  - design.leader-election => an alternative when the work is a singleton, not a pool
  - design.graceful-shutdown => terminate finishes the leased item before exiting
  - design.observability => backlog, in-flight, lease expiries per worker
  - k8s.deployment => workers are a normal stateless Deployment
  - k8s.hpa => scale from backlog, not CPU, when the work is IO-bound
  - rust.tokio-semaphore => in-process bound that matches the lease budget
  - rust.tokio-spawn => per-item task with explicit error reporting
failure_modes:
  - leases shorter than processing time, so work is duplicated instead of retried
  - scaling on CPU while the backlog grows (workers are IO-bound)
  - no dead-letter queue, so one poison item blocks a partition forever
  - no drain on SIGTERM, so every deploy redelivers in-flight items
follow_ups:
  - how do you keep total database connections bounded while scaling workers?
related:
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.idempotency
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/
